WEBVTT

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:03.911
Hi, this is a C++ program that I

00:00:03.911 --> 00:00:06.511
created called iMobile Bill Calculator.

00:00:07.151 --> 00:00:09.071
Basically, the program calculates your

00:00:09.071 --> 00:00:10.511
monthly phone bill based on the

00:00:10.511 --> 00:00:12.672
subscription package you choose from and

00:00:12.672 --> 00:00:15.352
how many gigabytes of data you use. There

00:00:15.352 --> 00:00:17.752
are three different package choices. As

00:00:17.752 --> 00:00:20.113
you can see, there's package A, package

00:00:20.113 --> 00:00:22.993
B, and package C, and each one has a

00:00:22.993 --> 00:00:24.473
different monthly price and data

00:00:24.473 --> 00:00:26.614
allowance. And I'm going to be showing

00:00:26.614 --> 00:00:28.134
you how each package works.

00:00:29.574 --> 00:00:31.894
So first things first, I'm going to start

00:00:31.894 --> 00:00:34.535
by selecting package A by

00:00:34.535 --> 00:00:35.255
entering 1.

00:00:37.975 --> 00:00:40.336
The program then asks the user how many

00:00:40.336 --> 00:00:42.136
gigabytes of data were used.

00:00:43.656 --> 00:00:46.616
Package A costs $39 a month and

00:00:46.616 --> 00:00:49.207
includes 4 gigabytes of data. And if you

00:00:49.207 --> 00:00:51.127
go over that amount, you get charged an

00:00:51.127 --> 00:00:53.207
additional $10 for each GB.

00:00:54.007 --> 00:00:56.048
So for this example, I'm going to be

00:00:56.048 --> 00:00:57.448
entering 6 GB.

00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:04.409
Since that's 2 GB over the limit, the

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:06.809
program adds an extra $20 to the monthly

00:01:06.809 --> 00:01:09.690
price. And that's

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.490
how the program works when the user

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:13.940
chooses package A.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.811
Next, I'm going to be trying package B

00:01:18.691 --> 00:01:19.891
by entering 2.

00:01:22.771 --> 00:01:25.452
Package B costs $59 a month and

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:28.212
includes 8GB of data. And if you go

00:01:28.292 --> 00:01:30.292
over that amount, each additional GB

00:01:30.292 --> 00:01:32.853
costs $5. So for

00:01:32.853 --> 00:01:35.173
example, let's say the user enters

00:01:35.173 --> 00:01:36.213
10GB.

00:01:40.294 --> 00:01:41.814
Since that's two gigabytes over the

00:01:41.814 --> 00:01:43.774
limit, the program adds an extra $10. And

00:01:45.294 --> 00:01:47.295
that's why the total bill comes out to

00:01:47.655 --> 00:01:49.335
$69.99. And

00:01:50.535 --> 00:01:52.535
that's how the program works for

00:01:53.256 --> 00:01:54.056
package B.

00:01:56.536 --> 00:01:59.056
Finally, I'm going to try package C by

00:01:59.056 --> 00:01:59.897
entering 3.

00:02:03.737 --> 00:02:06.617
Package C costs $69.99 a

00:02:06.777 --> 00:02:09.538
month and includes unlimited datawhich

00:02:09.538 --> 00:02:11.058
means you won't get charged extra no

00:02:11.058 --> 00:02:14.018
matter how many gigabytes you use. So for

00:02:14.018 --> 00:02:16.019
this example I'm going to be entering 15

00:02:16.019 --> 00:02:16.739
gigabytes.

00:02:21.539 --> 00:02:23.060
And as you can see the total is still

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.660
$69.99 because package C

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.140
includes unlimited data, which package A

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.741
and package B didn't include, which is

00:02:30.741 --> 00:02:33.301
why the price stays the same. And

00:02:33.301 --> 00:02:36.061
that's pretty much how my program works.

00:02:36.462 --> 00:02:37.262
Thank you for watching.
